: ","decorations":[{"type":"BOLD","fontWeightValue":700}]}},{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"The CBSE is expected to release the exact details of the new system, including the specific exam dates and guidelines, closer to the start of the 2024-25 academic year.","decorations":[]}}],"style":{},"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}}}],"metadata":{"version":1,"createdTimestamp":"2024-04-15T19:52:15.625Z","updatedTimestamp":"2024-04-15T19:52:15.625Z","id":"19ed69ac-6af1-418c-a8d3-1c6de9e9f2f1"},"documentStyle":{}},"contentText":"Good news!  No more facing board exams just once a year! The Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) is introducing a big change. Starting from the academic year 2024-25, there will be CBSE board exams twice a year for Class 10 and 12! Why the Change? This change aligns with the government's new education policy (NEP) 2020. The aim is to reduce exam stress and make learning more important. Benefits for Students: Less Stress:  Two chances to perform well means less pressure to excel in just one exam. \n Improved Learning:  With exams twice a year, you might focus on studying continuously throughout the year instead of cramming at the end. \n Second Chance:  If you don't score well in the first exam, you'll get another opportunity to improve in the second one. \n How Will it Work? The exact details are still being finalized, but here's what we know so far: Two Exam Windows:  Exams will likely be held twice a year, around November-December and February-March. \n Best Score Counts:  Your final result will likely be based on the higher score you achieve in either exam. \n Optional Second Exam:  There are reports that taking the second exam might be optional. If you're happy with your first score, you might not have to take it again. \n What This Means for You This new system gives you more flexibility and potentially less stress. Here are some things to keep in mind: Study Planning:  You can now plan your study schedule differently for each exam window. \n Focus on Understanding:  With the pressure of one exam reduced, you can focus on understanding concepts rather than just memorizing things. \n Time Management:  With two exams a year, you'll need to manage your time effectively to be prepared for both. \n A New Experiment This change is a big step for Indian education. It remains to be seen how successful it will be in reducing stress and promoting better learning. One thing's for sure – the upcoming academic year will be a time of adaptation and new experiences for CBSE students! FAQs Question: Will the new system affect my decision to change schools in Class 12? Answer: Yes, the new system might influence your decision. If you're considering changing schools, you'll need to factor in the potential for differences in curriculum and exam patterns between the two schools. Question: Can I still take the board exams just once a year? Answer: Yes, it's likely that you will still have the option to take the board exams just once a year. However, the exact details of this option are yet to be finalized. Question: How will the new system affect my overall percentage? Answer: Your final result will likely be based on the higher score you achieve in either exam. This means you can improve your overall percentage if you perform better in the second exam. Question: When will the exact details of the new system be announced? Answer: The CBSE is expected to release the exact details of the new system, including the specific exam dates and guidelines, closer to the start of the 2024-25 academic year."}